\chapter{Tutorial: Course content}
\label{tutorial-course-content}

[[course-content]] covers the part of a course that is not assignments:
modules, wiki pages, announcements and the calendar.
Its object of learning is that the [[list]]-and-[[view]] pattern from
the assignments tutorial (\cref{tutorial-assignments-and-grading})
holds for every kind of content, and that [[view]] prints text a
program can save, edit and send back.

The critical aspects are these:
\begin{description}
\item[The same pattern on new nouns] [[modules]], [[pages]] and
  [[discussions]] each have [[list]] and [[view]]; meeting the pair
  three times is what turns it from a fact into a pattern.
\item[Selectors as flags versus selectors as arguments] Most verbs
  select an item with an option such as [[-p]]; [[modules]] takes its
  regex as a positional argument and [[discussions]] takes the kind of
  item as one.
  The tutorial points these out where they occur, so the learner reads
  the help rather than assuming.
\item[Output as a file] What [[pages view]] prints is a Markdown file
  with a header that identifies the page, which is the basis of editing
  a page locally and pushing it back.
\end{description}

The arc is:
\begin{enumerate}
\item list and view the modules (steps 1--2);
\item list and view the pages (steps 3--4), then save a page to a file
  (step~5);
\item list and view the announcements (steps 6--7);
\item list the calendar (step~8);
\item name the verb that prints a page (step~9).
\end{enumerate}

As in the earlier tutorials, shell steps validate the typed command,
not the output, and no step uses shell hooks.
Step~5 writes a file, which is why the tutorial runs in a scratch
workspace of its own rather than in the learner's current directory.

\section{Tutorial source}

<<[[course-content.md]]>>=
---
id: course-content
title: Course content
summary: List and read modules, pages, announcements and the calendar, and save a page to a file.
---

<<list modules step>>

<<view modules step>>

<<list pages step>>

<<view a page step>>

<<save a page step>>

<<list announcements step>>

<<view an announcement step>>

<<list the calendar step>>

<<name the page verb step>>
@

\section{Step 1: See the structure of a course}

Modules are how a course is organised for the students, so they are
the first thing to list.
[[modules list]] requires [[-c]], unlike the verbs in the earlier
tutorials where it had a default; the pattern requires it too, so a
learner who leaves it out meets the error and the instruction in the
same step.

<<list modules step>>=
# See the structure of a course

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+modules\s+list\b
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+modules\s+list[^\n]*\s-c\b
hint: "modules has a list verb, and it requires -c."
```

Run `canvaslms modules list -c "<regex>"`, selecting one of your
courses as before.

Each row is one module: the course, the module name, when it unlocks,
whether it requires sequential progress, and how many items it holds.
Modules are the structure students see on the course's front page.

Exit the shell when you have the list.
@

\section{Step 2: Look inside the modules}

[[view]] prints the items inside each module.
This is also where the tutorial contrasts the two ways a verb selects
an item: [[modules view]] takes a regex for the module as a positional
argument, where [[assignments view]] took [[-a]].
The step does not require the regex, since viewing all modules is
useful, but the instructions show where it goes.

<<view modules step>>=
# Look inside the modules

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+modules\s+view\b
hint: "Same command with view instead of list. A module regex, if you want one, is a positional argument here rather than an option."
```

Run `canvaslms modules view -c "<regex>"`.

Now each row is one item inside a module: the course, the module, the
type of item (a page, an assignment, a file, a link), its name, and
what a student must do with it. To see one module only, add a regex
for its name as an argument after the options, for example
`canvaslms modules view -c "<regex>" "Week 1"`. Note the difference
from `assignments view`, where the assignment regex is an option,
`-a`; the help of each verb says which form it takes.

Exit the shell when you have seen the items.
@

\section{Step 3: Find the wiki pages}

The pattern repeats on pages, with [[-c]] optional again.
Pages have a title and a URL slug, and the slug is what a browser
shows; the row prints both so the learner can recognise a page they
know from the browser.

<<list pages step>>=
# Find the wiki pages

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+pages\s+list\b
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+pages\s+list[^\n]*\s-c\b
hint: "Same pattern, new noun: pages list -c."
```

Run `canvaslms pages list -c "<regex>"`.

Each row is one wiki page: the course, the title, whether it is
published, and its URL. Pick a title for the next step.

Exit the shell when you have the list.
@

\section{Step 4: Read one page}

[[view]] with a selector, as in step~2 of the assignments tutorial;
here the selector is [[-p]] and matches the title or the URL slug.
Requiring [[-p]] keeps the output to one page.

<<view a page step>>=
# Read one page

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+pages\s+view\b
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+pages\s+view[^\n]*\s-p\b
hint: "view with -p and a regex that matches one page title from the list."
```

Run `canvaslms pages view -c "<regex>" -p "<page>"`, where `<page>` is
a regex matching one title from step 3.

The page is printed as Markdown, converted from the HTML Canvas
stores, with a short header first: the title, the course and a
`regex` line that identifies the page. Keep reading; the next step
uses that header.

Exit the shell when you have read the page.
@

\section{Step 5: Save the page to a file}

The same command as step~4 with its output sent to a file, then
[[head]] on the file.
This is the contrast the tutorial was built around: nothing about the
command changed, only where the output went, and what went there is a
complete file that [[pages edit -f]] can push back to Canvas after the
learner edits it.
The header's [[regex]] line is what makes that round trip work, since
it identifies the page even if the title is changed in the file.
The step requires both the redirection and the [[head]], so that the
learner has looked at what was saved; it describes [[pages edit]] but
does not run it, since that writes to Canvas.

<<save a page step>>=
# Save the page to a file

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+pages\s+view[^\n]*>\s*\S+
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)head\b[^\n]*page\.md
hint: "Redirect the previous command into page.md with >, then run head page.md."
```

Run the same command as in step 4 with its output redirected to a
file, `canvaslms pages view -c "<regex>" -p "<page>" > page.md`, and
then run `head page.md`.

The file starts with a YAML header, between two `---` lines, and the
Markdown of the page follows. The `regex` line in the header
identifies the page, so if you edit the file and run
`canvaslms pages edit -c "<regex>" -f page.md`, the edited text goes
back to the same page even if you changed its title. That is the
whole workflow for keeping course pages in version control: view to a
file, edit, commit, edit back. This tutorial stops before the last
command, since it changes the page in Canvas.

Exit the shell when you have seen the header.
@

\section{Step 6: Read the announcements}

Announcements and discussions share the [[discussions]] verb, and the
kind of item is a positional argument: [[announcements]] or
[[discussions]].
This is the third selector form the learner meets, after options and
the positional regex of [[modules]], and the reason the tutorial
insists on reading the help.
The pattern requires the word [[announcements]] after [[list]].

<<list announcements step>>=
# Read the announcements

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+discussions\s+list\s+announcements\b
hint: "discussions list takes the kind of item as an argument: announcements or discussions. Then -c."
```

Run `canvaslms discussions list announcements -c "<regex>"`.

Announcements and discussions live under the same verb, and which of
them you want is the first argument: `announcements` here, or
`discussions` for the discussion forums. Each row is one announcement
with its title and date.

Exit the shell when you have the list.
@

\section{Step 7: Open the latest announcement}

[[view]] on the same argument prints the newest announcement in full.
Only the verb varies from step~6, which completes the third
list-and-view pair of the tutorial.

<<view an announcement step>>=
# Open the latest announcement

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+discussions\s+view\s+announcements\b
hint: "view instead of list, same argument, same -c."
```

Run `canvaslms discussions view announcements -c "<regex>"`.

By default `view` shows the most recent announcement, title and
message. To see more of them, add `-n` with a number; to pick one by
title, add `-t` with a regex. This is the third time you have swapped
`list` for `view`, and it works the same way on every kind of content.

Exit the shell when you have read the announcement.
@

\section{Step 8: What is coming up}

The calendar is the last noun, and it breaks the pair on purpose:
[[calendar list]] has no [[view]], and [[-c]] is optional because a
calendar without a course is the learner's own.
The step keeps [[-c]] so that the output stays within the course the
tutorial has been using.

<<list the calendar step>>=
# What is coming up

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+calendar\s+list\b
hint: "calendar list, with -c to stay within your course."
```

Run `canvaslms calendar list -c "<regex>"`.

Each row is one event: title, start and end time, and participants.
Assignment deadlines appear as events too. Without `-c` the command
lists your own calendar across all courses, and `--start-time` and
`--end-time` limit the range.

Exit the shell when you have the list.
@

\section{Step 9: Which command prints a page's text?}

The closing question asks the learner to pick the verb that produced a
file in step~5 from four verbs that all print something about pages
or modules.
The distractors are real commands they ran or could run, so the
answer requires the [[list]]-versus-[[view]] distinction and the noun
to be held at once.

<<name the page verb step>>=
# Which command prints a page's text?

```tutorial-step
kind: single_select
options:
  - pages list
  - pages view
  - modules view
  - quizzes list
answers:
  - pages view
hint: "Which verb printed the Markdown you saved in step 5?"
```

Which command prints the text of a wiki page?

`list` gives you rows to pick from, and `view` gives you the content
of what you picked, for pages, modules, assignments and announcements
alike. One noun this tutorial did not cover is quizzes:
`canvaslms quizzes list -c "<regex>"` lists them, and
`canvaslms quizzes -h` shows what else can be done with them.
@
